  annotation "note_1771579455341_0", anchor: "codified The Way∴al", text: "In the Way∴al entry I attributed this codification to Qeihong — fragments circulating in guild-hall rhetoric given doctrinal shape by an Exchange functionary who believes none of it. Here I give the authorship to ☽'Iarlocias and ☽'Saetacaeia-Viamana~. Both entries cannot be right as written. Having gone back to the deep-gallery impressions: Qeihong codified the *Aurora Stack export* — the version stripped of blood-oath framework and repackaged as guild-compatible reform rhetoric. What ☽'Iarlocias produced was the source text. Two codifications of identical raw material by parties with incompatible aims. I failed to distinguish them in either entry.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771579455341_1", anchor: "a ritual at the Bastion Apex", text: "The Wake-Singers also desecrated this site. Two unrelated cults performing blood-rites at the same crystalline structure, and no one in this edition — myself included — thought to connect them until now.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771579455341_2", anchor: "Neither faction has returned to verify", text: "Then the purists' certainty that something woke is faith, not observation. They are a cult; that is permitted. But I wrote this as though it were a factual dispute between two honest empirical positions. It is not. One side claims knowledge it cannot possess. The other stayed away, which is at least epistemically consistent.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771579455341_3", anchor: "the founding impulse was commercial rather than mystical", text: "Every faction on this Berg rewrites its origin story within a generation. Aureate buried its Still Pools desperation under crystalline ambition. The Exchange buried its three-family merger under institutional gravitas. The Communion buried a merchant's bid to break a trade monopoly under liturgy and blood-scripture. The pattern is so uniform it should be boring by now. It is not boring. It is the most reliable thing in the archive.", type: :commentary, display: :full
